  [38;5;153m(recipient_id, event_id, read_at, delivered_channels)[39m. Inbox is an indexed lookup on [38;5;153mrecipient_id[39m.
  - Buys: trivial fast reads; per-recipient read state is natural; [1mthe notification row is the delivery unit[0m, so the
    email/push channel we agreed to leave room for becomes "find rows where [38;5;153mdelivered_channels[39m lacks email" rather than
    new machinery.
  - Costs: write amplification (20 participants = 20 rows — irrelevant at this app's scale), and widening the notifiable
    set is not retroactive. Fan-out must be in the same transaction as the event write or notifications get lost on
    crash.
  - Rules out: nothing we care about.

  [1mC. Local-first, synced client.[0m Tasks and events in IndexedDB, synced via a thin server; notifications computed in the
  browser. [3mDiscarding this:[0m out-of-band email fundamentally needs a server that holds the state, and shared multi-user
  tasks add conflict resolution — it pays complexity on an axis we don't need.

  [1mD. Event-sourced with a queue and workers.[0m [3mDiscarding this:[0m it's where B goes if this ever gets big, and B's
  [38;5;153mnotification[39m table is the seam you'd put the queue behind. Building it now is YAGNI against an app that is currently
  11 lines of HTML.

  [1mI recommend B.[0m The deciding factor is the commitment we already made — in-app first, email/push later over a channel
  interface. B's notification row [3mis[0m that interface; A reaches the same place by growing per-recipient tables one at a
  time. The retroactivity B gives up is a genuine loss, but a small one: the full [38;5;153mevents[39m log is still there, so a
  one-off backfill can materialize history if you ever widen the notifiable set.
